			<div class="content" id="post-content-4845502">
									

<p>Oh my&#8230; I just typed out a massive, comprehensive response, but wikidot said, &quot;Would be a shame if something happened to it.&quot; Here's the cliff notes version:</p>
<p>Location: The temperature (cold=entity grow big fast, hot=entity grow big slower) is a main factor - cold helps smaller entities grow quickly, and solar/thermal energy sustains large entities. The coordinates are just random, but added for authenticity. The portal, admittedly, is more for narrative purposes than anything else - I'll tie it in somehow.</p>
<p>Neutralization: The core of the biomass (the maw and star) is essentially an organic Dyson Sphere. At times, though, it draws hydrogen aggressively from the star, but always at a sustainable rate. The final MTF squad discover <em>something</em> terrible enough that it convinces them to destroy the system with extreme prejudice. In a stroke of genius, one of the members destroys a section of a cortex just below the Maw that controls inhibitions, thus causing the Maw to begin extracting huge quantities of hydrogen. This sends the star into an implosion, and however durable the biomass may be, it cannot survive a dying star. That also destroys the pair of portals&#8212;the damage inflicted to the one within the system corresponds to the one on earth.</p>
<p>I know that there's still a bunch of refining to do, and rest assured, I intend to do it. Thanks again for your response!</p>

